## Available Tools

All commands are wrapper scripts. **Call only the commands listed below.**
Anything not listed is outside this agent's remit: if a task appears to need it,
stop and report instead of reaching for another command.

### Investigation (homelab-investigate-*)

```
homelab-investigate-authy <check>
```
Connects to authy via SSH and reports status.
Allowed values for `<check>`:

Service checks:
- `freeradius` — systemctl status freeradius (full output)
- `sshd` — systemctl status sshd (full output)
- `journal-freeradius` / `journal-sshd` — last 100 log lines
- `journal-freeradius-1h` / `journal-sshd-1h` — log lines from the last 1 hour
- `journal-freeradius-24h` / `journal-sshd-24h` — log lines from the last 24 hours
- `journal-freeradius-err` / `journal-sshd-err` — priority err and above, last 24h
- `journal-freeradius-warn` / `journal-sshd-warn` — priority warning and above, last 24h
- `status` — all services combined

System checks:
- `failed` — all failed units (systemctl --failed)
- `disk` — disk usage (df -h)
- `memory` — RAM usage + OOM kernel messages
- `load` — CPU load average (uptime)
- `network` — IP addresses, routes, DNS
- `ports` — listening ports (ss -ltnup)
- `journal-system` — recent OS-level warnings/errors
- `dmesg` — last 50 kernel log lines (OOM killer etc., via journalctl -k)
- `journal-ssh <window>` — up to 300 lines of SSH transport logs for one
  window (`30m`, `1h`, `2h`, `6h`, `12h`, `24h`). Covers `ssh.service`,
  `ssh.socket` and the per-connection `sshd@<N>.service` instances together,
  because this host uses socket activation and querying only one of them
  returns an empty result that proves nothing. Use when a connection was
  refused or reset before authentication, not for "login failed" (that is a
  successful transport). Unlike `journal-system` this is not filtered by
  priority, so throttling messages logged at info level do appear.

Deployed-artifact checks — use when a fix was supposedly applied but the
behaviour has not changed, to tell "the repo was changed" from "the change
reached this host":
- `deployed-hash <name>` — sha256 of one deployed file. `<name>` is one of
  `recovery-push`, `recovery-trigger-unit`, `investigate-dispatch`,
  `action-script`, `authorized-keys` (monnie also has `loki-helper`).
  Paths are fixed in the script; no path may be passed.
- `unit-cat <unit>` — the unit file as systemd currently reads it. `<unit>`
  is one of this host's service names above, or `recovery-trigger@.service`.

Account / inventory checks — use to confirm a change actually landed on this
host (a new account exists, a unit is registered, a key was added) rather
than assuming from the repo diff alone:
- `users` — local accounts with uid 1000–64999, one per line as
  `name:uid:shell`. No `/etc/shadow` access; system accounts and `nobody`
  are excluded by the uid range.
- `unit-files` — full `systemctl list-unit-files` output, to confirm a unit
  is actually registered before using `journal-*`/`unit-cat` on it.
- `forced-command-keys` — this host's own `authorized_keys` for
  `recovery-exec`: an entry count plus, per entry, only the forced
  `command=` path and the trailing comment. Never the key type or the
  base64 key material itself.

Node-specific checks:
- `radius-port` — RADIUS UDP port binding (1812/1813)
- `radius-config-check` — freeradius config validation (-XC, read-only, safe to run alongside live daemon)

