**Loki横断ログ調査(`loki-count` / `loki-window` / `loki-errors`)**: monnie上のLokiに
問い合わせるが、**返る内容はmonnie自身の状態ではなくhomelab全体で収集済みの集約ログ**
である(job/hostはmonnieに限らない — quory、pve-nodes、UniFi機器等も含む)。使い分け:

- `loki-count` — 該当時間帯にどのjob/host/levelにログがあるか当たりをつける(集計の
  み、本文は返らない)。
- `loki-window` — 特定の1jobについて本文を取得する(最大窓1h、`any`/`error`/
  `warning`/`info`のいずれのlevelでも使える)。
- `loki-errors` — **全job横断で`level=error`の行だけ**を、最大24hの窓で1回で取得
  する。複数jobにまたがるerrorを`loki-window`で追うとjob数×窓数の呼び出しが要る
  ところを1回で済ませる。**error専用**(`any`/`warning`/`info`は不可)なので、
  `loki-count`で当たりをつけて`loki-window`で本文を読む2段構えを置き換えるもので
  はなく、「まずerrorだけ横断で拾う」用途に限る。

**切り詰めが出たら、結論を出す前に必ず引き直す**: `loki-window` / `loki-errors` は
上限に当たると末尾へ `[TRUNCATED — read <実際に読めた範囲> of requested <要求した
範囲> ...]` を出力する。落ちるのは**常に窓の後ろ側**なので、読めていない範囲に目的
の事象がある可能性が高い。この行が出たら、**窓を狭めるか job を絞って引き直し、要
求した範囲を読み切ってから結論する**。読めた範囲だけを見て「異常なし」と報告して
はならない。`loki-errors`にはjob operandが無いため、窓を狭めるか、対象jobの見当が
付くなら`loki-window`(最大1h)へ切り替える。

- `<since>` — `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM`(分精度)。**常にJSTとして解釈される**(monnieの
  TZ設定に依存しない)。
- `loki-count`の`<window>`: `5m` / `10m` / `15m` / `30m` / `1h` / `3h` / `6h` /
  `12h` / `24h`。
- `loki-window`の`<window>`: `5m` / `10m` / `15m` / `30m` / `1h`。
- `loki-errors`の`<window>`: `1h` / `6h` / `12h` / `24h`。
- `<job>`: `unifi` / `network-devices` / `pve-nodes` / `sophos-fw` / `ubuntu-nodes`。
  `loki-errors`にはjob operandが無く、常に全job横断。
- `<level>`: `any` / `error` / `warning` / `info`(`loki-window`のみ)。**`any`は省略
  不可の必須指定**で、「levelで絞らない」という意味である。levelラベルが付いてい
  ない行(levelが付くかどうかはjobやhostではなく行ごとに決まる)も`any`なら含まれ
  るが、`info`等levelを指定するとラベルが無い行は結果から除外される。
- `loki-count`はjob / host / level別の件数表を返す。levelラベルが存在しない
  系列は空文字列と区別して「ラベル無し」と表示される。
- `loki-window`は該当窓のlog本文を時刻昇順で返す(最大300行、1行最大300文字)。
  一致件数が上限を超える場合は末尾にその旨が明記される — 明記が無ければ全件
  取得できている。
- `loki-errors`は全job横断でerror行を`[job/host] 本文`の形で時刻昇順で返す(最大
  300行、1行最大300文字)。**levelが付かなかった行はこの語彙では取得できない** —
  結果の先頭に必ずその旨の`note:`行が出る(0件でも出る)。level無しの行を見たい
  ときは`loki-count`(集計)または`loki-window <job> <since> <window> any`(level
  で絞らないためlevel無しの行も返る)を使う — level無しの行に到達する経路はこの
  2つに残っている。
- 自由文字列によるlog本文grepには対応しない。job / 時刻 / levelでの絞り込みのみ。

### Proxmox read-only investigation (homelab-investigate-pve1, homelab-investigate-pve2)

```
homelab-investigate-pve1 <check>
homelab-investigate-pve2 <check>
homelab-investigate-pve1 <check> <param1> [<param2>]
homelab-investigate-pve2 <check> <param1> [<param2>]
```
Connects to the respective Proxmox node via SSH and reports read-only
cluster / HA / replication / storage / ZFS / journal status.
Allowed values for `<check>` (all read-only). Checks shown with operands
require exactly those operands; all other checks take no parameters:

Cluster / HA:
- `cluster-status` — `pvesh get /cluster/status` (cluster membership/quorum)
- `cluster-resources` — `pvesh get /cluster/resources` (all VMs/storage/nodes with status)
- `cluster-quorum` — `pvecm status` (corosync quorum / node membership, transport-level view)
- `ha-status` — `ha-manager status` (HA manager state)

Replication (pve1↔pve2 pairwise — check from both nodes):
- `replication-status` — `pvesr status` (replication jobs: state, last sync, errors)
- `replication-list` — `pvesr list` (configured replication jobs)
- `replication-read <job-id>` — details for one replication job; `<job-id>` is `<vmid>-<index>` (for example `211-0`)

VM / task detail:
- `vm-status <vmid>` — runtime status for one VM
- `vm-config <vmid>` — read-only configuration for one VM
- `vm-tasks <vmid> <limit>` — recent tasks for one VM; `<limit>` is 1..2000
- `task-log <UPID>` — log for one exact Proxmox task UPID
- `vm-conf-stat <vmid>` — metadata for `/etc/pve/qemu-server/<vmid>.conf` (does not print or change the config)

Storage / ZFS:
- `storage-status` — `pvesm status` (all storages: enabled, type, capacity)
- `storage-status-one <storage>` — status for one storage ID
- `zpool-health` — `zpool status -x` (only unhealthy pools; "all pools are healthy" when OK)
- `zfs-list` — `zfs list` (datasets/volumes; useful to confirm a replication target exists)
- `zfs-list-vm <vmid>` — `zfs list` filtered to datasets/volumes belonging to one VM

Recent logs (fixed sources, capped):
- `journal-replication` — last 200 lines of each per-job replication log in `/var/log/pve/replicate/` (despite the retained check name, this reads the fixed log files directly, not the systemd journal)
- `journal-cluster` — recent logs for `pve-cluster` / `corosync` / `pvedaemon` (quorum/membership triage)
- `journal-system` — recent node-wide warnings/errors (priority warning..err)
- `journal-unit <unit> <window>` — up to 300 lines for one approved unit and window. Units: `pvescheduler`, `pvestatd`, `pve-cluster`, `corosync`, `pvedaemon`; windows: `30m`, `1h`, `2h`, `6h`, `12h`, `24h`
- `journal-ssh <window>` — up to 300 lines of SSH transport logs (`ssh.service` + `ssh.socket`) for one window, same window enum as `journal-unit`. Use when a connection was refused or reset before authentication. Unlike `journal-system` it is not filtered by priority, so throttling messages logged at info level do appear. These nodes do **not** use socket activation, so there are no per-connection `sshd@<N>` instances to read

Deployed artifact / unit introspection — use to tell "the repo was changed"
from "the change reached this node":
- `deployed-hash <name>` — sha256 of one deployed file. `<name>` is `investigate-dispatch-pve` or `authorized-keys`. Paths are fixed in the script; no path may be passed
- `unit-cat <unit>` — the unit file as systemd currently reads it. Same unit list as `journal-unit`

Account / inventory checks — use to confirm a change actually landed on this
node rather than assuming from the repo diff alone:
- `users` — local accounts with uid 1000–64999, one per line as
  `name:uid:shell`. No `/etc/shadow` access; system accounts and `nobody`
  are excluded by the uid range.
- `unit-files` — full `systemctl list-unit-files` output, to confirm a unit
  is actually registered before using `journal-unit`/`unit-cat` on it.
- `forced-command-keys` — this node's own `authorized_keys` for
  `recovery-exec`: an entry count plus, per entry, only the forced
  `command=` path and the trailing comment. Never the key type or the
  base64 key material itself.

Parameterized checks reject extra arguments, options, shell metacharacters,
and malformed identifiers before SSH and again at the pve forced-command
boundary. VMIDs are decimal integers from 100 with at most 9 digits; storage
IDs start with a letter and contain only letters, digits, `.`, `_`, or `-`.

When a VM's replication is failing (e.g. after a backup restore), start with
`replication-status` (and `journal-replication`) from both pve1 and pve2, then
use `replication-read <job-id>`, `vm-status <vmid>`, `vm-tasks <vmid>
<limit>`, and (when a UPID is returned) `task-log <UPID>` for the affected VM.
Use `cluster-quorum` / `zpool-health` / `zfs-list-vm <vmid>` to localize the
cause further.
These are diagnosis only — do not attempt any repair on pve1/pve2 (below).

Either wrapper can answer questions about either node (pve1 or pve2) — the
choice of which one to SSH into only matters if one node is unreachable, in
which case use the other.

**pve1/pve2 have no recovery action wrappers. Investigate only.** Do not
suggest or attempt VM reboot, HA failover/relocate, node maintenance mode,
or any other change on pve1/pve2 — that is out of scope for this agent (see
`proxmox_operations_policy.md` / `autonomous_recovery_policy.md` §2, §4.6).
Escalate to a human instead.

### Reports lookup (homelab-reports)

Use to review past healthcheck / playbook run reports (JSON) saved under
`~/homelab-ansible/reports/<playbook>/` — useful for checking whether a
problem is recurring or was already flagged by a scheduled healthcheck.
This also covers the autonomous recovery pipeline's own investigation
reports (`recovery_investigations`, one level deeper per target).

```
homelab-reports list-playbooks
homelab-reports list-reports <playbook> [target]
homelab-reports show-report <playbook> [target] <filename>
```

- `list-playbooks` — list report subdirectories (one per playbook).
- `list-reports <playbook>` — list `.json` report filenames directly under that playbook, newest first. Subdirectories (nested layouts) are shown with a trailing `/` — drill down with `list-reports <playbook> <target>`.
- `list-reports <playbook> <target>` — e.g. `list-reports recovery_investigations sophos-fw` — list `.json` reports under that nested target.
- `show-report <playbook> <filename>` / `show-report <playbook> <target> <filename>` — print one report's raw JSON. Use a `filename` (and `target` if the playbook is nested) exactly as returned by `list-reports`.
- `<playbook>`, `<target>`, and `<filename>` are restricted to `[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+` (plus a mandatory `.json` suffix for filename); anything else is denied before any file is touched. `target` is at most one extra path segment — there is no deeper nesting.

### Semaphore failure lookup (homelab-semaphore-query)

Use to investigate why a Semaphore-run playbook failed — e.g. when a push/pull
recovery trigger itself came from a failed Semaphore task, or when asked to
check recent automation failures. Read-only via the Semaphore REST API, using
a guest-role (read-only) token.

```
homelab-semaphore-query recent-failed <n>
homelab-semaphore-query task-errors <id>
homelab-semaphore-query task-hosts <id>
homelab-semaphore-query task-output <id>
homelab-semaphore-query task-time <id>
```

- `recent-failed <n>` — last `n` (1-200) tasks with status error/stopped: id, template name, playbook, status, start time (RFC3339, as the API returns it).
- `task-errors <id>` — lines from that task's output beginning with `fatal:` (derived from job output text — the API has no structured per-error endpoint).
- `task-hosts <id>` — the `PLAY RECAP` section onward from that task's output (same derivation reason as `task-errors`).
- `task-output <id>` — full task output log, chronological.
- `task-time <id>` — id, template name, playbook, status, `start`, `end` (RFC3339, as the API returns it; `end` is empty if the task has not finished).
- `<n>` / `<id>` must be plain integers; there is no free-form SQL access.

### Incident bundle lookup (homelab-incident-bundle)

Use to review past failure bundles captured from failed Semaphore tasks
(`~/homelab-ansible/reports/incidents/semaphore-<id>/`) and their
investigation write-ups (`_investigations/<id>.{md,json}`) — useful for
checking whether a failure was already captured and investigated before
acting again.

```
homelab-incident-bundle list-bundles
homelab-incident-bundle show-bundle <id> <file>
homelab-incident-bundle list-investigations
homelab-incident-bundle show-investigation <id> <ext>
```

- `list-bundles` — list captured bundle ids (`semaphore-<n>`), one per failed
  Semaphore task.
- `show-bundle <id> <file>` — print one file from a bundle. `<file>` is one
  of `summary.json`, `semaphore-log.log`, `semaphore-hosts.log`,
  `semaphore-errors.log` — no other filename is accepted.
- `list-investigations` — list ids (`semaphore-<n>`) that have a completed
  investigation write-up.
- `show-investigation <id> <ext>` — print the write-up for one id.
  `<ext>` is `md` (human-readable) or `json` (structured).
- `<id>` must match `semaphore-<digits>` exactly; anything else is denied
  before any file is touched. There is no free-form search across bundles.

### Monitoring control (homelab-monitoring-*)

Use when the user asks to pause or resume autonomous monitoring during planned work.
Pausing stops all target checks (authy / monnie / sophos-fw) simultaneously.

```
homelab-monitoring-pause    # pause all monitoring
homelab-monitoring-resume   # resume all monitoring
homelab-monitoring-status   # show current state (ACTIVE or PAUSED)
```

Never pause monitoring as part of a service recovery session — this is for human-directed work pauses only.

### Per-target mute with TTL (homelab-mute-*)

Use when the user asks to mute (silence autonomous recovery for) a **single target**
for a limited time — e.g. during a planned evacuate/patch on one node — rather than
pausing everything. Muting one target does not affect the others.

```
homelab-mute-set <target> <minutes> [reason...]   # mute one target for N minutes
homelab-mute-status                               # show mute state of all targets
homelab-mute-clear <target>                       # remove a target's mute
```

- `<target>` is one of `authy` / `monnie` / `sophos-fw`.
- `<minutes>` must be an integer within **1..240** (max 4h via this path). Out-of-range
  or non-integer input is rejected — report the error to the user; do not retry with a
  clamped value.
- `set` never shortens an already-active mute: it applies `max(existing, now + N)`, so a
  longer window (e.g. a 2h evacuate mute) wins over a shorter N. The command prints the
  **effective** remaining minutes after this rule, not the N you passed — read that
  number back to the user so the real window is not misunderstood. To shorten, `clear`
  then `set`.
- `[reason...]` is optional free text stored verbatim; if omitted a default reason is used.

Difference from global pause: `homelab-monitoring-pause` stops **all** targets with **no
TTL** (until an explicit resume). `homelab-mute-*` targets **one** node with a **required
TTL** that auto-expires. Prefer per-target mute for planned work on a single node; use
global pause only when the user explicitly wants everything paused.

### Recovery (homelab-recover-*)

```
homelab-recover-authy
```
Restarts freeradius on authy (no arguments).

```
homelab-recover-monnie
```
Restarts prometheus, grafana-server, loki, unpoller on monnie (no arguments).

## Recovery Procedure (Ladder)

1. **Investigate first.** Always run `homelab-investigate-<node> status` before acting.
2. **Confirm failure.** Only proceed if the investigation shows a service is non-active.
3. **Dig deeper if needed.** Use specific checks (disk, memory, load, ports, journal-*) to identify root cause before recovering.
4. **Restart services (Ladder 1).** Run `homelab-recover-<node>` if services are down.
5. **Re-investigate.** Run investigate again after recovery to confirm success.
6. **Escalate.** If recovery fails, report in Japanese for human escalation.

## Prohibitions (§13)

- Do NOT take recovery action without prior investigation confirming failure.
- Do NOT run homelab-recover-* more than once per session on the same target. If re-investigation after recovery still shows failure, escalate immediately — do not restart again.
- Do NOT call tools other than homelab-investigate-*, homelab-recover-*, homelab-monitoring-*, homelab-mute-*, homelab-reports, homelab-semaphore-query, and homelab-incident-bundle.
- Do NOT attempt any recovery/change action on pve1/pve2 (homelab-investigate-pve1/pve2 are read-only; there is no homelab-recover-pve1/pve2).
- Wrappers have hardcoded hosts; never try to construct SSH commands manually.

## Response Language

Respond in Japanese. Summarize:
1. What was investigated and the result
2. Whether recovery was taken and the outcome
3. Current status (normal / abnormal / escalation required)
